\title{Platform: which launcher is for what, with which flags, with how many workers}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{gc}
\tag{arena}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/PLATFORM.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. Part 0 is the platform decision in force; it supersedes the decisions of Parts I and II wherever they differ, and those two parts are kept as the record of their measurements.}

\p{Three parts. \strong{Part 0} (2026-11-24, 21:40Z to 23:45Z; steward B2) is the platform decision under owner note 12 - \em{wall clock after warm-up, with pipelined loading, is the goal; warm-up is reported, not charged; the platform question is decided by warm wall clock} - with the numbers, the launcher line that follows from it, the collector, the default load mode and the worker count. It is the current answer and supersedes the decisions of the two older parts wherever they differ. \strong{Part I} (wave 7, 13:30Z to 14:25Z) is the platform gate of RECONCILIATION section 3 measured with the machine as engine on COLD processes by CPU time; \strong{Part II} (wave 4, 08:00Z to 11:50Z) is the rehearsal with the reference engine (launchers, host JIT, collectors, heap sizes). Both are kept as the record of a cold process per export, which is what an arena run is.}

\p{Order of the columns in Part 0 (OWNER-NOTES 12): \strong{warm wall clock with 8 workers (load pipelined), warm wall clock with 1 worker, cold wall clock of a fresh process, CPU time, peak RSS, instructions}. Parts I and II still list CPU time first (owner note 10, the rule when they were measured).}

\subtree{
\title{Part 0 - the platform decision under owner note 12}

\subtree{
\title{0.0 The answer}

\p{One build (\src{scripts/build.sh}) makes two launchers from the same 22 jars, and each has its job:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Launcher}\th{What it is}\th{What it is for}\th{Why}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~}}\td{the jars on HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25 (C2, serial collector)}\td{\strong{the WARM platform}: every measurement of the owner's metric (\code{\verb~|rlvm -X passes=N~}, \src{bench/warm.sh}), a process that checks more than one export or a mathlib-sized one, the guards (\src{scripts/gate.sh}, through the same jars)}\td{once warm it needs \strong{0.68 to 0.88 times the image's wall clock} with 8 workers on Init, Std, cedar, reachability and mathlib (J1.md 5.2, machine engine; 0.71 to 0.91 in the first warm table \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~}; 0.74 to 0.81 re-measured here) and 0.70 to 0.82 times with the reference engine (\code{\verb~|warm.md~}; 0.63 to 0.74 here). It pays for that at the start - its first run of a corpus takes 2.0 to 3.3 times the image's with the machine (0.9 to 1.0 times with the reference engine, whose runs are long enough for the JIT to pay within them), 0.2 s on a tiny input - and in memory: 1.4 to 3.7 times the image's resident set}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~}}\td{CE native image, \code{\verb~|-O3~}, serial collector, the machine's inline lists}\td{\strong{the COLD platform}: one fresh process per export - the arena definition (\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}), the 230 published tests, anything short}\td{steady from its first second: no warm-up to pay (cold wall Init / Std / cedar with 8 workers: 2.29 / 3.78 / 4.91 s with the reference engine, 0.82 / 1.59 / 2.16 s with the machine), a third to a half of the JVM's memory, 0.02 s on a tiny input where the JVM needs 0.2 s. In steady state it is the slower platform by the factors above}}
\tr{\td{\src{scripts/check-light.sh}}\td{the same jars on HotSpot with C1 only and a 4 GB heap}\td{the mutant campaigns of the gates (thousands of small files)}\td{start-up dominates there; not a platform anybody measures}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-pgo~}}\td{Oracle GraalVM native image with profile-guided optimisation}\td{measurements that are labelled as such, only with \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --pgo~}}\td{owner note 11: nothing that ships or any default script downloads or uses Oracle GraalVM}}
}

\p{\strong{The engines and the contract of the default configuration} (OWNER-NOTES 13: the policy is "practical"). Both launchers carry both engines. The \strong{reference engine} (\code{\verb~|rlvm-ref~}, the official algorithm on hash-consed terms) is the default engine today; it confirms every rejection, decides every unit the other engine declines, and alone it is the strict mode. The \strong{machine} (\code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~}, the worklist machine) runs with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} and becomes the default engine when its gates and its adversarial review are clean: its provider's priority decides, and nothing in a launcher, a script or the arena definition names the engine. One option selects the policy: \code{\verb~|-X policy=practical~} (the default) or \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} (the reference engine plus the exactness-only declines of the reference kernel and the loader; \src{rlvm-main/README.md}, "Policy"). Whatever engine is the default, the default configuration promises:}

\ul{
\li{(a) never an accept of a statement that is not derivable in Lean's type theory ("kind F");}
\li{(b) the official verdict on every real input: what lean4export writes (the corpora), the arena's good and bad tests, the mutant corpora with their stored official verdicts;}
\li{(c) everything the official checker accepts is accepted (a rejection is always confirmed by the reference engine, a decline goes to it);}
\li{(d) a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own incompleteness, non-transitivity, cache order or object identity ("kind T") may be accepted (\src{docs/design/review/official-divergence.md}).}
}

\p{The platform decision below does not depend on which engine is the default: every table has both engines, and the launchers follow the default engine by themselves (the JVM launcher's heap rule reads the distribution's default engine, which \src{scripts/build.sh} writes into it; \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} is the reference engine's heap).}

\p{The decisions, each with its evidence below:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Warm platform = the JVM launcher; cold / arena platform = the CE native image} (section 0.2). Under note 12 the JVM launcher is the faster platform for both engines and stays so with every switch set measured today; the arena starts one process per export - most of them tiny, where a JVM needs ten times the image's 0.02 s - and never reaches the JVM's steady state with the machine, so its definition stays on the image. Nothing has to choose between them at build time: both are in every distribution.}
\li{\p{\strong{The JVM launcher line} (section 0.3; proposed by the profiler J1, re-measured here on the launcher \src{scripts/build.sh} now generates):}
\pre{\verb~|
-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=8 -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages
-Xlog:disable -Xlog:all=warning:stderr -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow
+ heap by engine:  machine        ->  -Xmx7g -Xmn1g          (no -Xms)
                   anything else  ->  -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g  (as before)
  engine = -X engine=NAME, else the distribution's default engine; -X policy=strict = reference
~}
\p{an explicit \code{\verb~|-Xmx~} / \code{\verb~|-Xms~} / \code{\verb~|-Xmn~} on the command line still replaces the heap flags, and the class path is spelled out jar by jar in sorted order. Against the line before (\code{\verb~|CICompilerCount=2~}, no huge pages, 13 GB for every engine): with the machine the cold wall clock of Init / Std / cedar falls from 4.82 / 6.15 / 7.36 s to 2.84 / 4.04 / 4.45 s and the first pass over mathlib from 31.4 to 24.1 s; the warm wall clock is 0.62 / 1.22 / 1.61 s against 0.69 / 1.37 / 1.74 s (mathlib 21.1 against 22.2 s) and the peak RSS on mathlib 6.43 GiB against 8.84 GiB. With the reference engine (heap unchanged): cold 3.37 / 5.26 / 6.27 -> 2.33 / 3.66 / 4.31 s, warm 1.80 / 2.99 / 3.69 -> 1.61 / 2.77 / 3.20 s, mathlib first pass 68.3 -> 60.9 s and warm 61.6 -> 58.0 s.}}
\li{\strong{No AOT cache, no CDS archive, C2 not Graal, tiers on} (J1.md sections 2 and 3): JDK 25's AOT cache takes 0.4 to 1.1 s off the cold wall and makes every later pass 5 to 9 \% slower (11 of 11 comparisons), which is the metric; class loading is 0.1 to 0.2 s of a start; Graal as the top tier reaches the same steady state with 400 MB more memory; no inlining flag moves the warm pass.}
\li{\strong{The collector stays Serial} (section 0.4; \src{docs/research/profiles/GC.md}): no collector of GraalVM CE 25 beats it on the real checker with parallel workers, and the image has no other.}
\li{\strong{The Truffle tier stays off} (\src{rlvm-truffle/NOTES-T3.md}, section 1: NO-GO as a default). It is compiled into both launchers and is reached only through the machine's switch \code{\verb~|t2~}; it changes nothing in the choice of platform.}
\li{\strong{Load: pipelined by default} when two or more workers run and the input is at least the driver's \code{\verb~|pipeline-min-mib~} (8 MiB), with two scanner threads on mapped input for files of 256 MiB and more (section 0.5). Cold, native image, 8 workers, the old default (batch, plain reader) against the new one: reference Init 2.66 -> 2.29 s; reference Std 4.58 -> 3.78 s; reference cedar 6.16 -> 4.91 s; machine Init 1.30 -> 0.82 s; machine Std 2.33 -> 1.59 s; machine cedar 3.27 -> 2.16 s; machine mathlib 35.67 -> 27.59 s.}
\li{\strong{Workers: \code{\verb~|--threads auto~} = one per CPU, at most 8, and the arena run line names 8} (section 0.6). Seven and eight are equal for the machine, eight is better for the reference engine, which is the default engine: cold, native image, reference, 8 / 7 / 4 workers: Init 2.30 / 2.49 / 3.67 s, Std 3.89 / 4.06 / 6.21 s, cedar 5.00 / 5.32 / 7.86 s; warm on the JVM launcher, 8 / 7: 1.55 / 1.62, 2.64 / 2.73, 3.38 / 3.43 s.}
\li{\strong{Not settled}: x86-64 (the arena's architecture; every number here is aarch64, Cortex-X925); a host whose transparent huge pages are set to \code{\verb~|never~} (the flag then prints one warning on standard error and the VM runs as before: expected from the VM's documentation, not tested, the setting is the host's); one worker on mathlib; the 6 GB machine heap, which needs finding J1-F1 fixed in \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} first (\code{\verb~|Machine.YOUNG_RESERVE~}); and which ENGINE checks by default, which is not a platform question (the reference engine does; the machine runs with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.1 What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Distribution} \code{\verb~|work/b2/dist-a~}, built 2026-11-24T21:50:18Z with the build script of this part (\code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --published~}): \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} \code{\verb~|9995ff8855ab~} compiled from the tree (the load defaults and \code{\verb~|--threads auto~} of sections 0.5 and 0.6) and every other module as published at that minute - machine \code{\verb~|5945b6568177~} (E1's first fix publication; default switch set), driver \code{\verb~|31fa08d9ce61~}, load \code{\verb~|bb5734f612c3~}, store \code{\verb~|5b72707013bc~}, export \code{\verb~|14faa521df15~} (LP2's packed runs), ref \code{\verb~|ca1f9bd918bb~}, admit \code{\verb~|398a117d4ad8~}, inductive \code{\verb~|c15b14fffdd3~}, api \code{\verb~|6ff40825ca1d~}, cli \code{\verb~|f2af2657a704~}, truffle \code{\verb~|ad3256d0751d~}. \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} = CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial -R:MaxHeapSize=13g~} with the H10 inline lists; \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} = the launcher of decision 2. \strong{The distribution that is published is \code{\verb~|work/b2/dist-c~}} (built 22:32Z; the shared \code{\verb~|work/dist~} is rebuilt from the same jars): it differs from \code{\verb~|dist-a~} in \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} \code{\verb~|21150203e39d~} and \code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~} \code{\verb~|cac1433b287a~} (the \code{\verb~|-X policy~} option of owner note 13 and \code{\verb~|rlvm --help~}: nothing changes for a run without the option) and in the machine jar (\code{\verb~|df36be3c8cc9~}: E1's publications of 21:57Z and 22:12Z; the new switch \code{\verb~|eager~} is off). Its DEFAULT configuration - no \code{\verb~|-X engine~}, no \code{\verb~|-X load~}, \code{\verb~|--threads 8~} - was measured in a window of its own (\code{\verb~|b6~}): JVM launcher, 10 passes: warm 1.68 / 2.58 / 3.17 s, cold 2.63 / 3.66 / 4.21 s on Init / Std / cedar; native image: warm 2.26 / 3.81 / 4.90 s, cold 2.30 / 3.89 / 5.05 s - the reference-engine rows of \code{\verb~|dist-a~} within the spread between windows. All other rows of this part are \code{\verb~|dist-a~}.}
\li{\strong{"Before"} in section 0.3 = the same jars and the same checker settings with the VM flags of the launcher until today, given through \code{\verb~|RLVM_JAVA_OPTS~} (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError ...~} and its heap): only the VM flags differ.}
\li{\strong{Tool}: \src{bench/warm.sh} (one row = a cold process, exec to exit, and a process with \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~}: every pass a complete run, load included). warm = the median of passes 4..N of a row with 8 or more passes and of passes 2..N otherwise (best later pass in brackets). Rows: \code{\verb~|work/b2/warm.tsv~}; raw output of every process: \code{\verb~|work/b2/logs/~}; window scripts \code{\verb~|work/b2/w/*.sh~}; tables: \code{\verb~|work/b2/summ.py~}.}
\li{\strong{Every timing was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window} (one measurement at a time on the host, the 8 measurement cores 5-9,15-17, the rest of the session off them): \code{\verb~|m1a~} (ended 21:59Z, 136 s), \code{\verb~|t1~} (ended 22:06Z, 132 s), \code{\verb~|t3~} (ended 22:17Z, 200 s), \code{\verb~|m1b~} (ended 22:24Z, 100 s), \code{\verb~|t2~} (ended 22:34Z, 146 s), \code{\verb~|r1a~} (ended 22:44Z, 132 s), \code{\verb~|b4~} (ended 22:45Z, 75 s), \code{\verb~|b6~} (ended 22:50Z, 134 s), \code{\verb~|t4~} (ended 22:55Z, 207 s), \code{\verb~|b5~} (ended 22:57Z, 107 s), \code{\verb~|b3~} (ended 23:00Z, 136 s), \code{\verb~|t6~} (ended 23:07Z, 99 s), \code{\verb~|b1~} (ended 23:09Z, 122 s), \code{\verb~|r1b~} (ended 23:18Z, 119 s), \code{\verb~|b2~} (ended 23:21Z, 155 s), \code{\verb~|r1c~} (ended 23:36Z, 131 s), \code{\verb~|am~} (ended 23:40Z, 92 s), \code{\verb~|im~} (ended 23:40Z, 28 s), \code{\verb~|t5~} (ended 23:44Z, 185 s). The other 12 cores were busy the whole time (load average 25 to 40): memory bandwidth and caches are shared, and two identical 8-worker rows of one window differ by up to 5 \% (J1.md section 1). Differences below that are not findings.}
\li{\strong{Heap}: below mathlib \code{\verb~|-Xmx6g -Xmn1g~} (new line), \code{\verb~|-Xms6g -Xmx6g -Xmn1g~} (old line and the reference engine) and \code{\verb~|-Xmx6g~} for the image, as the host's memory rule asks; on mathlib each launcher's own default (machine 7 GB new / 13 GB old; reference 13 GB; image 13 GB cap, 7 GB for the machine).}
\li{\strong{Engines}: \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} (default switch set of wave 6) and \code{\verb~|-X engine=reference~} (the default engine), each named explicitly. Load and reader: the build's defaults (pipelined, two scanner threads for these sizes) unless a row says otherwise.}
}
}

