\p{Reading. Warm, with 8 workers, the JVM launcher needs 0.74 to 0.81 times the image's wall clock with the machine and 0.63 to 0.74 times with the reference engine: the same picture as J1's 0.68 to 0.88 and the first warm table's 0.70 to 0.91, on newer jars, for both engines and on every corpus. Cold, with the machine, it is the other way round: the JVM's first run costs 2.0 to 3.3 times the image's. With the reference engine the two first runs take about the same time below mathlib (0.86 to 1.01 times): its runs are long enough for the JIT to pay within them, and the new launcher line took a third off the JVM's start. The JVM holds 1.4 to 3.7 times the memory. So the answer to "which platform" is the answer to "is warm-up charged": under owner note 12 it is not, and the JVM launcher is the platform of the metric; for one process per export it is - most of the arena's exports are tiny, where a JVM needs 0.2 s against the image's 0.02 s, and with the machine the image is two to three times faster cold on the corpora too - and the image is the platform of the arena.}

\p{Earlier measurements of the same question, for the record: J1.md 5.2 (machine \code{\verb~|8a8ea5644b24~}, windows A, B, 4): warm wall JVM / image 0.79 (Init), 0.80 (Std), 0.76 (cedar), 0.68 (reachability), 0.88 (mathlib) with 8 workers, 0.67 to 0.87 with one; cold 3.0 / 1.75 / 2.1 / 0.97 / 0.93 (first pass). \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~} (machine \code{\verb~|7eb33a7a4ad7~}, reference \code{\verb~|ca1f9bd918bb~}): machine 0.90 / 0.91 / 0.84 / 0.71 / 0.73, reference 0.73 / 0.70 / 0.74 / 0.82 on Init / Std / cedar / reachability (/ mathlib). INT6 (\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-integration.md~}, machine \code{\verb~|78c74ca92785~}): mathlib 21.2 s on the JVM launcher against 27.4 s on the image. Part I of this document (cold processes, CPU time): the image needs 0.68 / 0.46 / 0.37 times the JVM launcher's CPU time with 1 / 4 / 8 workers - the same two platforms seen from the other side: the JVM's first pass costs several times a warm one.}
}

