\p{Reading. The cold wall clock is where the line shows: 0.59 to 0.66 times the old line's on Init, Std and cedar with the machine (0.69 to 0.70 with the reference engine), 0.77 on the first pass over mathlib: the eight compiler threads. With the machine the old line's pass 2 is still 1.6 to 2.8 times a warm pass and its pass 3 1.3 to 1.9 times; the new line's pass 3 is at 0.98 to 1.11 times its steady state. Warm, the new line is at 0.89 to 0.95 times the old line with the machine and 0.87 to 0.94 with the reference engine: J1 could claim no steady-state gain below mathlib (0.88 to 1.04) and 6 \% on mathlib from the huge pages; these rows are single windows, so read them as "not slower, a few per cent faster". Memory: the machine's 7 GB heap takes 2.4 GiB off the resident set on mathlib at the same wall clock; below mathlib the resident set is the touched part of the young generation and the store, as before.}

\p{What each change is for (J1.md has the experiments; this table is the re-measurement of the whole line):}

\table{
\tr{\th{Change}\th{Why}\th{Evidence}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|CICompilerCount~} 2 -> 8}\td{with 2 a tiered VM has ONE C2 thread beside eight workers that still run interpreted and C1 code; it needs five or six passes to become steady. The threads are created on demand: 8 is a cap}\td{J1.md 3.2: Init cold 5.3 -> 2.9 s, cedar pass 1 7.4-8.9 -> 5.0 s, pass 3 steady; here: machine cold 4.82 / 6.15 / 7.36 -> 2.84 / 4.04 / 4.45 s (Init / Std / cedar), pass 3 0.69 / 1.20 / 1.66 s against 0.87 / 2.56 / 2.36 s; reference cold 3.37 / 5.26 / 6.27 -> 2.33 / 3.66 / 4.31 s}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|+UseTransparentHugePages~}}\td{the one flag with a consistent steady-state gain: TLB reach for a store of gigabytes}\td{J1.md 2.2: mathlib -6 \% warm wall and -9 \% first pass in an A B A B; reachability -2 to -4 \%; cedar 0 to -4 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-Xlog:disable -Xlog:all=warning:stderr~}}\td{the VM prints its own warnings on standard OUTPUT by default (huge pages unavailable, ...)}\td{J1.md 3.1}}
\tr{\td{machine: \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}, no \code{\verb~|-Xms~}}\td{the same warm wall clock as 13 GB with 2 GB less resident memory; 7 GB is the smallest cap at which the machine decides every unit it decides at 13 GB}\td{J1.md section 4: 23.9 / 24.1 / 24.2 / 24.0 s for 13 / 8 / 7 / 6 GB, RSS 8.0-8.7 -> 5.7-6.7 GB; here: mathlib, machine, old line with 13 GB against new line with 7 GB: warm pass 22.2 against 21.1 s, peak RSS 9052 against 6587 MiB}}
\tr{\td{reference and every other engine: \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~} unchanged}\td{the reference engine's garbage on mathlib fills whatever heap it is given (13.4 GB resident, \code{\verb~|warm.md~}); the one alternative that showed something is another collector, not another size (section 0.4)}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{sorted, explicit class path}\td{\code{\verb~|lib/*~} is expanded by the VM in directory order, which is not the same on every file system; with it the order of the \code{\verb~|ServiceLoader~} providers}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{not added: AOT cache}\td{0.4-1.1 s off the cold wall, 5-9 \% ON every warm pass}\td{J1.md 3.3}}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.4 Collectors: Serial stays}

\p{\src{docs/research/profiles/GC.md} measured every collector of GraalVM CE 25 in JVM mode on the real checker, both engines, 1 / 4 / 8 workers, Init, Std, the Cedar slice, reachability and mathlib:}

\table{
\tr{\th{ratio to Serial, CPU time (geometric mean of Init and Std)}\th{machine, 1 worker}\th{machine, 8 workers}\th{reference, 1 worker}\th{reference, 8 workers}}
\tr{\td{Parallel}\td{0.99}\td{1.00}\td{1.01}\td{1.03}}
\tr{\td{G1}\td{1.00}\td{1.00}\td{1.00}\td{1.01}}
\tr{\td{ZGC (generational)}\td{1.04}\td{1.07}\td{1.06}\td{1.06}}
\tr{\td{Shenandoah}\td{1.15}\td{1.17}\td{1.21}\td{1.19}}
\tr{\td{Shenandoah, generational}\td{1.15}\td{1.16}\td{1.19}\td{1.26}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Wall clock with 8 workers: no collector improves it} (every ratio at or above 1 but G1's 0.99 on Std with the reference engine; ZGC and Shenandoah 1.2 to 1.3). The most a perfect collector could remove is Serial's pause total: 0.1 to 0.35 s on Init / Std, 1.2 s of 90 s (machine) and 7.3 s of 270 s (reference) on mathlib. In warm passes (\code{\verb~|warm.md~}): Parallel and G1 are inside the run-to-run spread of Serial on Init, Std and cedar (30 to 50 ms of pauses per warm pass).}
\li{There is nothing to collect: with the machine no collector runs a single collection on Init, one on Std, nine on mathlib; Epsilon, which never collects, is not faster than Serial.}
\li{The one cell where another collector has something: mathlib with the REFERENCE engine and 8 workers, G1 -3 to -6 \% CPU and a peak RSS of 7.1 GB against 12.4 GB (the 105 MB survivor spaces of \code{\verb~|-Xmn1g~} overflow at every young collection). Not adopted: one corpus, the engine that confirms rather than the one meant to ship, and G1 costs on the small corpora. If the reference engine stays the default engine for long, this is the first thing to measure again (with \code{\verb~|-XX:SurvivorRatio~} for Serial).}
\li{The image has no choice: GraalVM CE's native image has the serial collector (and epsilon). Its own finding (P7-F4, 18:14Z) is open: \code{\verb~|-Xms6g~} for the image on mathlib with 8 workers pipelined took the collections from 359-379 to 26 and the wall clock from 34.1 to 32.5 s (-4.7 \%, two interleaved rounds) for 0.8 GB more resident memory; in a batch check phase it gains nothing. Not baked in (\code{\verb~|-R:MinHeapSize~}): it is a mathlib-only gain measured on an older driver, and the arena's small tests would each reserve what they do not use. To be re-measured on the current jars.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.5 The default load mode}

\p{\code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} (\code{\verb~|MainOptions.resolveLoadDefaults~}) now decides what the command line leaves open:}

\table{
\tr{\th{Setting}\th{Default}\th{Before}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X load~}}\td{\code{\verb~|pipelined~} when two or more workers run AND the input is a file of at least the driver's \code{\verb~|pipeline-min-mib~} (8 MiB) AND no \code{\verb~|stop-after~} phase is asked for; else \code{\verb~|batch~}. \code{\verb~|load=auto~} spells it out}\td{\code{\verb~|batch~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X scan-threads~}}\td{2 for inputs of 256 MiB and more (either load mode), else 0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|-X io~}}\td{\code{\verb~|auto~} (mapped if a regular file) when scanner threads run, else \code{\verb~|buffered~}}\td{\code{\verb~|buffered~}}}
}

\p{A setting that is given wins. \code{\verb~|--stats~} prints what was decided (\code{\verb~|load=pipelined(auto) scan-threads=2(auto) io=auto~}). Nothing changes for one worker, for a file below 8 MiB (every published arena test but the corpora, every mutant) or for a run that names its mode.}

\p{Why it may be the default: the driver decides in pipelined mode what it decides in batch mode (kept environment pass + parking rule, rlvm-driver \code{\verb~|150aed4bf1bf~} / \code{\verb~|31fa08d9ce61~}: 0 pipelined-only declines over 16,928 mutants x 9 configurations with either engine), both modes build the same store (\code{\verb~|PipelinedLoadTest~}, record by record), and the scanner threads with packed runs build it word for word (LP2's differential digest: identical in 14 reader configurations including mathlib). Gates of THIS change: section 0.7.}

\p{What it buys:}

\table{
\tr{\th{engine}\th{corpus}\th{old default (batch, plain reader): wall / CPU / RSS MiB}\th{new default (pipelined, 2 scanner threads): wall / CPU / RSS MiB}\th{new / old wall}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{init}\td{2.66 / 14.4 / 441}\td{2.29 / 14.7 / 523}\td{0.86}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{std}\td{4.58 / 24.4 / 626}\td{3.78 / 24.9 / 611}\td{0.83}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{cedar}\td{6.16 / 31.7 / 761}\td{4.91 / 32.5 / 837}\td{0.80}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{init}\td{1.30 / 5.2 / 319}\td{0.82 / 5.5 / 429}\td{0.63}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{std}\td{2.33 / 9.8 / 561}\td{1.59 / 10.3 / 719}\td{0.68}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{cedar}\td{3.27 / 13.5 / 658}\td{2.16 / 14.5 / 889}\td{0.66}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{mathlib}\td{35.67 / 185.8 / 3355}\td{27.59 / 196.0 / 4073}\td{0.77}}
}

\p{Reading. With the batch default the load is one thread and its wall clock is added to the check's; pipelined, the workers check while the file is read, and the two scanner threads take the tokenising and the packing of the proof records off the loader thread. New / old cold wall clock: reference Init 0.86, reference Std 0.83, reference cedar 0.80, machine Init 0.63, machine Std 0.68, machine cedar 0.66, machine mathlib 0.77. The gain is largest where loading was a large share of the run (the machine: loading was half of its cold wall clock on Init, Std and cedar) and smallest where checking dominates (the reference engine). Warm, on the JVM launcher, with 8 workers, the scanner threads by themselves move a pass by a few per cent at most (machine, 2 scanner threads against the plain reader: Init 0.64 against 0.70 s, Std 1.17 against 1.27, cedar 1.60 against 1.58; reference: 1.55 against 1.60, 2.64 against 2.74, 3.38 against 3.27 s): beside 8 workers on 8 cores a pass is bound by its total CPU time, as the load owner measured (the load alone: Std 0.92 -> 0.50 s, cedar 1.36 -> 0.73 s, mathlib 9.4 -> 6.0 s with two scanner threads; three are no better). They are the default because they are never worse and halve the load wherever cores are free (one worker, four workers, the first seconds of a run). What it costs: CPU time +2 to +7 \% and 0.1 to 0.7 GB of resident memory in the table above (the level snapshots of the publications, the packing on the scanner threads), and the arena's metric pays a little - instructions of the cold process in the arena container, image, 8 workers, old -> new default, one run each: machine Init 83.1 -> 84.7 G (+2.0 \%), cedar 209.5 -> 218.9 G (+4.5 \%); reference Init 245.7 -> 247.5 G (+0.7 \%), cedar 516.1 -> 525.5 G (+1.8 \%) (\code{\verb~|work/b2/instr-load.out~}, \code{\verb~|instr-load-old.out~}). \code{\verb~|-X load=batch -X scan-threads=0~} is the old default for whoever counts instructions.}
}

\subtree{
\title{0.6 Workers: 7 against 8 on 8 cores}

\p{\code{\verb~|--threads auto~} is decided in \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} now (\code{\verb~|RlvmChecker.autoWorkers~}: one worker per CPU, at most 8) and passed to the driver like an explicit count; the driver's own automatic rule (CPUs - 2, at most 6: chosen by CPU time in wave 4) is no longer consulted, and its cap of one worker per 128 units still applies. The question was whether the eighth worker should be left to the loader, the scanner threads and the environment thread:}

\table{
\tr{\th{platform, engine}\th{corpus}\th{reader}\th{8 workers: warm (best) / pass 1 / CPU}\th{7 workers: warm (best) / pass 1 / CPU}\th{4 workers}}
