\subtree{
\title{cold (a fresh process, exec to exit), native image, the same window as the warm rows above}

\table{
\tr{\th{engine}\th{corpus}\th{8 workers: wall / CPU / RSS MiB}\th{7 workers}\th{4 workers}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{init}\td{2.30 / 14.7 / 532}\td{2.49 / 14.7 / 475}\td{3.67 / 14.4 / 452}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{std}\td{3.89 / 25.1 / 655}\td{4.06 / 24.8 / 615}\td{6.21 / 24.5 / 561}}
\tr{\td{reference}\td{cedar}\td{5.00 / 32.9 / 805}\td{5.32 / 32.7 / 854}\td{7.86 / 31.8 / 713}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{init}\td{0.85 / 5.5 / 427}\td{0.83 / 5.5 / 416}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{std}\td{1.57 / 10.4 / 747}\td{1.61 / 10.3 / 713}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{machine}\td{cedar}\td{2.23 / 14.7 / 900}\td{2.18 / 14.5 / 843}\td{-}}
}

\p{Reading. \strong{Machine}: warm on the JVM launcher 8 against 7 workers 0.64 / 1.17 / 1.60 against 0.64 / 1.16 / 1.75 s on Init / Std / cedar, cold in the image 0.85 / 1.57 / 2.23 against 0.83 / 1.61 / 2.18 s: a tie inside the 5 \% between rows, as the first warm table found ("seven equal eight, six are slower"). On mathlib 8 against 7: 21.2 against 21.1 s warm on the JVM launcher, 28.2 against 29.1 s in the image. \strong{Reference engine} (the default engine): the eighth worker pays - cold in the image 1.04 to 1.08 times the 8-worker wall clock with 7 workers and 1.57 to 1.60 times with 4 (the arena run line until today), at the same CPU time; warm on the JVM launcher 7 workers are 1.01 to 1.05 times 8. The reference engine's passes are bound by the checking, which spreads over the workers: a warm Std pass on the JVM launcher keeps 7.2 of the 8 cores busy (19.1 s of CPU in 2.64 s), so a core that is left to the loader, its scanner threads and the environment thread is a core the checking misses. The load owner's windows agree (8 on 8 cores; 7 no better on Std and cedar, worse on mathlib and in the image; \src{rlvm-load/README.md}, "LP2: warm measurements"). So \code{\verb~|--threads auto~} is one worker per CPU up to 8 and the arena run line says 8. With the machine as the default engine the choice costs nothing; it should be measured again if the machine's passes get shorter than the load.}
}
}

