\tr{\td{Readiness for a machine-default tree: the same gate on a REHEARSAL distribution whose default engine is the machine (\code{\verb~|work/b2/dist-mdefault~}: the old machine jar \code{\verb~|78c74ca92785~} with priority 100, not for verdicts)}\td{the launcher reads \code{\verb~|default engine: machine~} and runs the 7 GB heap without an option; 230 / 230 with the machine checking by default; self-test caught; the hostile step FAILS that jar as it must: agree 395, kind T tolerated 6, \strong{wrong accepts 19} (the E1-F1 .. F7 rows that are not of kind T; fixed in the machine's later jars)}}
\tr{\td{The shared distribution after the publication (\code{\verb~|work/dist~} -> \code{\verb~|dist.d/20261124T231129Z-9cdeba20778f-native~}: the published jars of 23:08Z, loader \code{\verb~|7e3f85cecdaa~}, store \code{\verb~|30979ee659b7~}, machine \code{\verb~|97d17e57e51b~})}\td{the 230 published tests through the arena run line (image, \code{\verb~|--threads 8~}): 230 / 230, 0 declined (\code{\verb~|work/status/slice-all.md~}); \code{\verb~|-X policy=strict~} declines a definition with a metadata node around its value on both launchers (\code{\verb~|policy=strict[metadata]~}), the default accepts it}}
}

\p{Not gated by me: the mutant corpora and the invariance suite were not repeated on the jars that other owners published after 22:32Z (their owners gate their own publications); the hostile exports of S3, S2 and C1 are not in the manifest yet; nothing ran on x86-64.}

\p{\strong{Changed} (all in the steward's modules and scripts):}

\ul{
\li{\src{scripts/build.sh}: the JVM launcher template (decision 2: VM flags, heap by engine from \code{\verb~|-X engine=NAME~} / \code{\verb~|-Xengine=NAME~}, sorted explicit class path, VM messages on standard error); the test kit is never part of a distribution (a tree build - the arena build - used to put \code{\verb~|rlvm-testkit~} on the image class path: 23 jars against the 22 of every gated distribution); the CE image is built with the machine's H10 inline lists, generated from the machine jar of the build with the \code{\verb~|javap~} and \code{\verb~|jar~} of the build's own GraalVM (no \code{\verb~|unzip~}: the arena's shell has none), with a fallback to an image without them (\code{\verb~|--no-h10~}: never).}
\li{\code{\verb~|rlvm-main~}: \code{\verb~|MainOptions~} (\code{\verb~|load~} / \code{\verb~|scan-threads~} / \code{\verb~|io~} defaults as in 0.5; \code{\verb~|load=auto~}, \code{\verb~|scan-threads=auto~}), \code{\verb~|RlvmChecker~} (\code{\verb~|--threads auto~} = \code{\verb~|autoWorkers~}; the statistics line names what was decided), tests \code{\verb~|LoadDefaultsTest~}. \strong{\code{\verb~|-X policy=practical|strict~}} (OWNER-NOTES 13; \code{\verb~|PolicyTest~}): strict = the reference engine, \code{\verb~|ref.exact=official~} for the reference kernel and \code{\verb~|LoadOptions.withStrictMetadata(true)~} for the loader where the jars have them. \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} names the default engine; \code{\verb~|rlvm --help~} lists the checker's settings (\code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~}: \code{\verb~|Checker.usage()~}).}
\li{\src{scripts/gate.sh}, \src{scripts/slice.sh}, \src{scripts/check-light.sh}: \code{\verb~|--engine default~} / \code{\verb~|--load default~} (no \code{\verb~|-X~} at all), \code{\verb~|gate.sh --defaults~} (the distribution exactly as a user runs it, so the same command gates a reference-default and a machine-default tree; every oracle is an official verdict), \code{\verb~|gate.sh --hostile~} (\src{scripts/gate-hostile.py}: the hostile exports judged by their official verdicts, a kind T row may be accepted), lanes clamped by the memory available at the start of a step.}
\li{\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}: the state, the description, \code{\verb~|--threads 8~} / \code{\verb~|threads: 8~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|bench/track~} (rule on wall clock, pass lines, the generator of \src{docs/BENCHMARKS.md}), \src{bench/warm.py} (\code{\verb~|--load default~}, \code{\verb~|--cold-only~}, the distribution in every row).}
}

\p{\strong{Not changed}: which engine is the default (the reference engine; \code{\verb~|Engines.DEFAULT_ENGINE_MIN_PRIORITY~} = 100, the machine registers with 10); the image's collector, heap cap (13 GB) and optimisation level; the driver, the loader, the store, the engines.}

\p{\strong{To repeat} (each line one quiet window of two to four minutes; \code{\verb~|work/b2/w/win.sh NAME...~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- scripts/build.sh --published --dist work/b2/dist-a
bench/quiet.py -- bash work/b2/w/m1a.sh     # launcher line, machine, Init / Std / cedar      (m1b: mathlib)
bench/quiet.py -- bash work/b2/w/r1a.sh     # launcher line, reference                        (r1b, r1c: mathlib)
bench/quiet.py -- bash work/b2/w/t1.sh      # 7 against 8 workers, JVM warm, machine          (t2: reference)
bench/quiet.py -- bash work/b2/w/t3.sh      # 4 / 7 / 8 workers, native, cold + warm          (t4, t5: mathlib)
bench/quiet.py -- bash work/b2/w/t6.sh      # batch against the default load, cold
python3 -I work/b2/summ.py                  # the tables of this part
~}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{Part I - the machine as engine (wave 7): cold processes, by CPU time}

\p{\em{Superseded as the platform decision by Part 0 (owner note 12: warm wall clock decides). Kept as the record of what a fresh process per export costs on each launcher - the arena's situation - measured with the machine v0 on a batch load. "The launcher", "the flags" and "the worker count" in this part are the cold-process answers of 14:25Z; the current ones are in Part 0.}}

\subtree{
\title{I.0 The answer}

\p{\strong{Read this first.} This gate was planned and measured under owner note 10 (CPU time of a run first, then wall clock): every run below is a FRESH process on a batch load, so the JVM's warm-up is part of every figure. While it was being written the owner changed the target (OWNER-NOTES item 12, read here at 14:37Z): \em{wall clock after warm-up, with pipelined loading, is the goal; warm-up cost is not charged; the platform question is decided by warm wall clock}. By that criterion the four points below are the answer for a COLD process - which is what an arena run is - and \strong{the platform question itself is open again}: section I.7 has the first indication (the machine's warm passes on HotSpot need less CPU time than the image, as the reference engine's long runs do) and what is needed to decide it.}

\p{For a fresh process per export (the arena's run), by CPU time, then wall clock:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The CE native image is the launcher of a cold run} (\code{\verb~|work/dist/bin/rlvm~}, GraalVM CE 25, \code{\verb~|-O3~}), and the arena definition stays on it. With the machine as engine it needs \strong{0.68 / 0.46 / 0.37 times the JVM launcher's CPU time with 1 / 4 / 8 workers} (medians of 8 paired runs each on Init, Std, the Cedar slice and reachability; the image wins all 24 pairs, 0.24 to 0.88), 0.62 times its wall clock with four workers, and a third to a half of its memory. Init, four workers: 8.1 s CPU / 2.9 s wall / 330 MB against 18.4 s / 4.9 s / 770 MB. The JVM pays about 3.7 s of C2 compilation per run and runs every worker slowly until it is done; a run of the machine is too short to earn that back. The JVM launcher remains the cheaper platform for long runs of the \em{reference} engine (mathlib, eight workers: 500.5 s against 624.6 s CPU), which is the fallback engine and not what ships. \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml} already names the image: nothing to switch.}
\li{\strong{Flags: the image as it is built today} - \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial -R:MaxHeapSize=13g -R:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError~}, no nursery flag, no \code{\verb~|-march~}. \code{\verb~|-O3~} (10 to 18 \% less CPU time than \code{\verb~|-O2~} for the machine) was the arena build's level and is now also the default of \src{scripts/build.sh}. The serial collector is the only CE collector that collects; the heap limit is a limit, not a reservation (RSS 250 MB to 1.9 GB on the four corpora). The JVM launcher keeps its flags (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}); it is the launcher of the guards, not of the arena.}
\li{\strong{Workers: \code{\verb~|--threads 4~} by CPU time, 8 by wall clock; the run line was left at 4.} With four workers the image's CPU time is that of one worker (1.02, 1.01 and 0.98 times on Init, Std and the Cedar slice) at 2.5 to 2.7 times the speed; eight workers cost 1.07 times the CPU time of one on all three for 18 \% less wall clock than four on Init and Std and nothing on the other two corpora. Under note 10 that reads "four"; under note 12 (wall clock is the goal, CPU time a diagnostic) it reads "eight, or six once it is measured" (section I.3). The run line was not changed: all 230 published tests under the arena's limits with another count could not be run in this session.}
\li{\strong{What this rests on, and what it does not settle.} Two repeats of the matrix (48 runs, 13:33Z to 13:49Z, host load 26 to 95). The third repeat, the six-worker runs and the \code{\verb~|bench/track~} entries of 14:13Z to 14:21Z were taken while the host was thrashing (load 230 to 266, 40 GB of swap; orchestrator, \code{\verb~|work/status/requests.log~} 14:32Z) and are discarded; their repetition waited for the measurement lock behind eight other queues and was withdrawn at 14:34Z. Not settled: x86-64 (the runner's architecture; no such machine here); the machine on mathlib-sized input on either launcher (section I.4); the machine's staging switches and its compiled tier, which its own workflow is changing today (measured here: jar \code{\verb~|608b84b2b142~}, default switches).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{I.1 What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{Distribution} (frozen for the whole series): \code{\verb~|work/steward/w7/dist~}, built 13:30:43Z by}
\pre{\verb~|
scripts/build.sh --published --no-pgo --opt 3 --dist work/steward/w7/dist
~}
\p{from the jars published at that moment (\code{\verb~|BUILD-INFO.txt~} there): the pipeline jars of the end of wave 4 / 5 (driver \code{\verb~|eb0944caa630~}, store \code{\verb~|45df9b63e7af~}, ref \code{\verb~|035f31e854d9~}, admit \code{\verb~|a817cd0c9d35~}, load \code{\verb~|30617170f7a3~}, api \code{\verb~|46e5486d1a02~}) and the machine jar \strong{\code{\verb~|608b84b2b142~}} (the wave-4 integrator's final jar; the wave-6 machine workflow published newer ones from 13:39Z on, which are not in these numbers). \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} = CE native image, GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial -R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}, 64.9 MB: exactly what \src{scripts/arena/build.sh} builds. \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} = the same jars on HotSpot with the launcher's flags (\code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}).}}
\li{\strong{Engine}: \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} with the machine's default switches (v0: one session per declaration; the staging switches \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,probes~} of \code{\verb~|work/status/machine-integration.md~} are off). The reference engine re-checks what the machine declines; on the four corpora below it declines nothing (section I.6), so every figure is the machine's own.}
\li{\p{\strong{One run} = the arena run line with the binary and the worker count replaced, in a fresh arena-shaped container (\code{\verb~|bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network~}: 8 performance cores, 16 GB), measured like the arena measures (\src{bench/measure.sh}: \code{\verb~|perf stat~} + GNU time around \code{\verb~|sh -c~}):}
\pre{\verb~|
RLVM_STATUS_BASE=10 DIST/bin/BINARY --threads N "$IN" -X engine=machine -X load=batch; case $? in ...
~}
\p{One-worker runs are in the same eight-core container (not the two-core container of Part II).}}
\li{\strong{Matrix}: launcher \verb~|{~\code{\verb~|jvm~}, \code{\verb~|native~}\verb~|}~ x workers \verb~|{~1, 4, 8\verb~|}~ x corpus \verb~|{~Init 348 MB, Std 584 MB, the Cedar slice \code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~} 133 MB, reachability 19 MB\verb~|}~, the two launchers of a group back to back. \strong{Two repeats are valid} (48 runs, 13:33Z to 13:49Z). A third repeat and three runs with six workers for the image (36 runs, 14:13Z to 14:20Z) fell into the forty minutes in which the host was out of memory and thrashing; by the orchestrator's ruling they are discarded and kept apart (\code{\verb~|w7-machine-thrashing.jsonl~}), and section I.3 quotes them only to say what they cannot show. \src{scripts/platform-matrix.py} (\code{\verb~|work/steward/w7/queue.sh~} and \code{\verb~|queue2.sh~} have the exact calls); raw records with the host's load average before and after every run in \code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/results/w7-*.jsonl~}.}
\li{\strong{Lock}: every run was made under the measurement lock \code{\verb~|work/perf-history/.ladder.lock~} (the first six groups with a lock per group, the rest in holds of 4 to 8 minutes). The lock was the bottleneck of the day: up to ten queues behind it, waits of 20 minutes, once held for more than ten minutes by a native-image build that had inherited it (my line of 14:02Z in \code{\verb~|work/status/requests.log~}).}
\li{\strong{The host was not quiet.} The 1-minute load average of the host (20 CPUs) was 26 to 95 during the two valid repeats: thirty agents were testing, building and measuring. Other owners' processes are not confined to the cores outside the container, so CPU time is inflated by an amount that changes from minute to minute (Cedar slice, image, one worker: 3.60 and 3.64 s here against 3.17 s at load 7 to 10 two hours earlier, with the same 46.4 G instructions; Init 7.94 s against 7.77 s). The figure of a row is the \strong{minimum} over its repeats (median in parentheses); the \code{\verb~|Load~} column is the highest 1-minute load average at the end of a run of the row. Instruction counts do not depend on the load; compare launchers by the paired ratios.}
}
}

