}
% <!-- END ratio:w7-machine:jvm:-:8 -->

\p{Reading:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The CE native image needs less CPU time than the JVM launcher in every group} - every corpus, every worker count, both repeats (24 pairs). Median of the paired ratios: 0.68 with one worker, 0.46 with four, 0.37 with eight; the best case for the JVM is reachability with one worker (0.85 and 0.88), the worst Init with eight workers (0.24 and 0.34).}
\li{\strong{The JVM's price is its JIT, and it is paid per worker.} A run of the machine on Init is 8 s of CPU in the image. On HotSpot the same run is 12.8 s with one worker, 18.4 to 21.6 s with four and 24 to 34 s with eight: C2 needs about 3.7 s to compile the machine's 800 methods (\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-integration.md~}), and until it is done every worker runs interpreted and C1 code. The image's CPU time hardly moves with the workers (7.9 / 8.1 / 8.3 s).}
\li{\strong{Wall clock}: equal with one worker (median ratio 1.01: the JVM's compiler and collector threads run beside its one worker), 0.62 times with four workers and 0.57 with eight. Init with four workers: 2.9 s against 4.9 s; Std: 4.4 s against 6.8 s.}
\li{\strong{Peak RSS}: 0.35 to 0.42 times the JVM's (medians; Init, four workers: 330 MB against 770 MB; Std: 450 MB against 1.26 GB). \strong{Instructions}: 0.68 to 0.82 times.}
\li{\strong{This is the opposite of Part II's result for mathlib with the reference engine}, where HotSpot's code is better than the CE compiler's by 11 to 26 \% in CPU time and a run lasts ten minutes, so the warm-up is amortised. With the machine the runs are an order of magnitude shorter and its hot code is a few flat loops over \code{\verb~|int~} arrays, which the ahead-of-time compiler handles as well as C2: reachability, whose two heavy declarations run for seconds each (the closest thing to a long, hot run among the four), is still 0.73 to 0.88 times in the image's favour. Whether the JVM catches up on a mathlib-sized run of the machine is in section I.4.}
}
}

