}
% <!-- END scaling:w7-machine:-:1:1,4,8 -->

\p{Reading (the image; the JVM launcher pays 1.2 to 2.7 times its one-worker CPU time for eight workers and is not a candidate):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{More workers cost CPU time although they add no work.} The image executes the same instructions at every worker count (Init: 136.6 / 137.1 / 137.2 G with 1 / 4 / 8 workers). Four workers need +0 to +3 \% CPU time against one on Init, Std and the Cedar slice (Init +1.1 / +2.5 \%, Std +0.1 / +1.4 \%, slice -1.5 / -3.2 \% in the two repeats) and eight need +4 to +11 \% (Init +4.1 / +10.7 \%, Std +4.7 / +8.4 \%, slice +6.6 / +6.9 \%): the workers share the memory system, and the machine's time is hash-consing probes and array growth (the same effect as with the reference engine, \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}). \strong{The busier the host, the dearer a worker}: on Init and Std the repeat at the higher load (75 to 86 against 26 to 56) is the dearer one.}
\li{\strong{What they buy.} Four workers: 2.5 to 2.7 times the speed of one on Init, Std and the slice (2.8 and 3.0 on Init and Std in the quieter repeat). Eight workers: 18 to 19 \% less wall clock than four on Init and Std at their best (2.36 against 2.89 s, 3.60 against 4.43 s), nothing on the Cedar slice (1.41 s both), and reachability does not scale beyond 1.5 times at any count (two heavy declarations decide its wall clock; the scheduler's claim cut, \code{\verb~|work/status/integration.md~} "What is open").}
\li{\strong{The gate of \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}} ("the largest N <= 6 whose CPU time exceeds that of one worker by at most 5 \%"): four workers pass on Init, Std and the slice (1.02, 1.01, 0.98) and are just above it on reachability (1.06 in both repeats); eight fail on Init, Std and the slice (1.07 each) and pass on reachability (1.03).}
\li{\strong{Six workers} (the driver's proposal for \code{\verb~|--threads auto~}) have no valid measurement. The three runs per corpus that this series made with six workers (14:18Z to 14:20Z) are among the discarded ones: the host was at load 240 to 266 and swapping, Init with eight workers took 6.2 s of wall clock there against 2.36 s an hour earlier. For the record only, CPU seconds of those runs (minimum of three) beside the discarded third repeat's 1 / 4 / 8 workers of the same minutes: Init 8.57 (8.23 / 8.67 / 9.01), Std 14.57 (13.46 / 14.57 / 16.42), Cedar slice 3.83 (3.32 / 4.00 / 4.45), reachability 8.35 (8.43 / 8.72 / 8.74) - six cost what four cost and less than eight, on a host where four already cost +5 to +21 \% against one. That neither speaks against six nor confirms it.}
\li{\strong{Reading under the two criteria.} CPU time first (owner note 10, in force when this was measured): four is the largest measured count at which the image's CPU time is still that of one worker; eight buy 18 \% of wall clock on two of four corpora for 4 to 11 \% more CPU time on three of them - keep \code{\verb~|--threads 4~}. Wall clock first (owner note 12, since 14:37Z): the same numbers say eight - less wall clock on Init and Std, the same on the Cedar slice, no worse on reachability within its scatter, and CPU time is only a diagnostic. \strong{The run line was left at \code{\verb~|--threads 4~} (and \code{\verb~|threads: 4~}, the CPU slots the arena reserves)}: a change needs all 230 published tests under the arena's time and memory limits with the new count (the wave-5 check ran them with four), which did not fit into this session. Six is the candidate the driver's owner proposes (with the reference engine he measured 12 to 25 \% less wall clock than with four on Init, Std and cedar, \src{rlvm-driver/README.md}); it needs a quiet host and a mathlib run with the machine to be judged (his \code{\verb~|threads-gate.sh --native --engine machine~} on mathlib held the lock from 14:21Z on). Changing it later is two numbers in \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{I.4 mathlib (reference engine), one run per launcher}

\p{The reference engine did not change between the wave-5 check and this series (the same pipeline jars; \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~} differs only by the run-defaults plumbing of section I.8, which no engine uses), so the mathlib runs of 12:56Z and 13:03Z are the runs of this gate: one per launcher, eight workers, the \code{\verb~|-O3~} image, the same container, through \src{bench/track/track.py} (\code{\verb~|work/perf-history/mathlib.jsonl~}, note \code{\verb~|wave5-check~}; distribution \code{\verb~|work/pipeline-check/dist~}), at a host load of 9 to 14 - quieter than anything this afternoon offered (load 26 to 266).}

\table{
\tr{\th{Corpus}\th{Workers}\th{Launcher}\th{CPU s}\th{Wall s}\th{Peak RSS}\th{Instructions}\th{Load}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{8}\td{\code{\verb~|jvm~}}\td{\strong{500.5}}\td{97.4}\td{12.85 GiB}\td{5,971 G}\td{14}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{8}\td{\code{\verb~|native~}}\td{624.6}\td{120.3}\td{7.15 GiB}\td{8,533 G}\td{9}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, \code{\verb~|-X load=pipelined~}}\td{8}\td{\code{\verb~|jvm~}}\td{\strong{501.0}}\td{73.2}\td{12.90 GiB}\td{5,895 G}\td{13}}
\tr{\td{mathlib, \code{\verb~|-X load=pipelined~}}\td{8}\td{\code{\verb~|native~}}\td{605.2}\td{90.9}\td{6.76 GiB}\td{8,479 G}\td{13}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{For the reference engine on mathlib the JVM launcher still needs less CPU time}: 0.80 times the image's (0.83 pipelined), 0.81 times the wall clock, with 5.7 GiB more memory (its heap setting). The other large corpora of the same check: navier-stokes-euler 0.82 (175.3 against 214.6 s), cslib 0.93 (125.2 / 134.1 s), con-leche 1.01 (60.2 / 59.8 s). Part II measured 0.80 to 0.89 on mathlib with this morning's jars: the picture held while the engine became twice as fast.}
\li{\strong{What that decides: nothing for the shipped binary.} The reference engine is the fallback and the re-check engine; with the machine as primary engine it checks the inductive blocks and what the machine declines, and on every corpus it was run on to the end here the machine declines nothing (section I.6). A mathlib run of the reference engine is not what the arena will see.}
\li{\strong{A four-worker pair} (the run line's count) was queued (\code{\verb~|queue2.sh B~}) and never reached the measurement lock; Part II section 2.3 has the four-worker pair of this morning's jars (JVM 945.8 s, image 1,068.6 s: 0.89). \strong{No mathlib run of the machine was made in this series} (it was queued as \code{\verb~|queue2.sh D~} / \code{\verb~|F~}; the acceptance run of section I.6 is not a measurement).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{I.5 Heap and collector flags}

\p{\strong{The image} (what ships): \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial --no-fallback -R:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}, no \code{\verb~|-march~}, no nursery flag - what \src{scripts/build.sh} and \src{scripts/arena/build.sh} build today.}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|-O3~}: measured by the machine's integrator in this container two hours earlier (\code{\verb~|-O2~} against \code{\verb~|-O3~}, one worker): Init 9.61 -> 7.84 s CPU, Std 15.70 -> 12.82, Cedar slice 3.77 -> 3.21, reachability 9.15 -> 7.75 (-15 to -18 \%; an earlier pair of his series, on a busier host: Init 10.11 -> 9.08, Std 16.18 -> 14.85, slice 3.84 -> 3.59, -7 to -10 \%); reference engine Init 15.41 -> 13.82. \code{\verb~|-march=native~}: no change. It was the arena build's level already; since this wave it is \code{\verb~|build.sh~}'s default too.}
\li{Collector: CE offers the image \code{\verb~|serial~} and \code{\verb~|epsilon~}. How much there is to collect is visible on the JVM launcher, whose runs are logged (column "Collections" in I.2; 1 GB nursery): zero to one young collection per run on Init, Std and the Cedar slice, five on reachability, never a full one, 30 to 520 ms of pauses in runs of 8 to 37 CPU-seconds. The machine's working memory is \code{\verb~|int~} arrays that live as long as a session. The image's own collections were not recorded; \code{\verb~|epsilon~} (never collect) could save at most that order of magnitude and turns a mathlib-class input into an out-of-memory risk: not built, not measured.}
\li{Heap limit: \code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~} is a limit, not a reservation - the image's RSS is what it uses (Init 250 to 490 MB, Std 330 to 570 MB, reachability 1.3 to 1.9 GB above; mathlib with the reference engine 7.15 GiB). 13 GB leaves 3 GB of the runner's 16 GB and is above the memory account's 12 GiB decline, so an input that does not fit is declined, not killed.}
\li{Nursery (\code{\verb~|-Xmn256m~}, \code{\verb~|1g~}, \code{\verb~|2g~} against the default): queued (\code{\verb~|queue2.sh D~}), not measured - it never reached the measurement lock.}
}

\p{\strong{The JVM launcher} (not shipped; the launcher of guards and of \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh dist~}): flags unchanged, \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}. Part II sections 3 to 5 measured the alternatives with the reference engine (Serial against Parallel and G1: no CPU difference, more memory; C2 against Graal: none; HotSpot's own compiler thread count: 0.92 times the CPU time with eight workers; a 10 GB heap: no CPU cost). None of them can bring the JVM launcher near the image for the machine (0.37 to 0.68 above), so they were the last use planned for the lock (\code{\verb~|queue2.sh E~}) and were not measured with the machine.}
}

