}

\subtree{
\title{I.7 Owner note 12: warm wall clock - what this part does not answer, and a first indication}

\p{Note 12 asks every benchmark for, in this order: warm wall clock with 8 workers (pipelined), warm wall clock with 1 worker, cold wall clock of a fresh process (for information), CPU time, peak RSS, instructions; "warm" = the same process checks the same export again after a first pass. Against that:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Everything above is the third and fourth item of that list} (cold wall clock and CPU time of a fresh process, batch load). It stays true for what the arena does - one process per export - and it no longer decides which platform is the fast one.}
\li{\strong{The launcher cannot make a warm pass}: \code{\verb~|rlvm~} checks one export and exits. A \code{\verb~|--passes N~} (same process, per-pass wall clock and CPU time in the statistics, the passes must agree) belongs in \code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~} / \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~}; it changes the launcher's fail-closed path and could not be guarded in what was left of this session, so it is not there.}
\li{\p{\strong{A first indication, not a measurement of record} (\code{\verb~|work/steward/w7/warm/Warm.java~}: a harness that calls \code{\verb~|RlvmChecker.checkExport~} several times in one JVM; \code{\verb~|work/steward/w7/warm/init-1w.txt~}). Machine, Init, ONE worker, the two spare performance cores (CPUs 18, 19; not the container), 14:38Z to 14:42Z at a host load of 135 to 155 - the cores were shared, so the wall clock of these runs (17 to 62 s) says nothing and only the CPU time per pass is quoted:}
\table{
\tr{\th{Platform}\th{Pass}\th{CPU s}}
\tr{\td{JVM launcher's flags (C2, Serial, 6 GB heap), one process}\td{1 (cold)}\td{15.62}}
\tr{\td{}\td{2}\td{8.49}}
\tr{\td{}\td{3}\td{8.23}}
\tr{\td{}\td{4}\td{7.08}}
\tr{\td{CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3~}, fresh process}\td{-}\td{9.81}}
\tr{\td{CE native image \code{\verb~|-O3~}, fresh process}\td{-}\td{9.41}}
}
\p{\strong{Once warm, HotSpot needs 0.72 to 0.87 times the image's CPU time for the same check} (passes 3 and 4 against the image's two runs in the same minutes on the same cores), where its cold pass needs 1.6 times. That is the reference engine's picture on mathlib (0.80) repeated for the machine: the JIT's steady-state code is better than the CE ahead-of-time compiler's. One corpus, one worker, disturbed cores (the image's run costs 7.94 s in the container; here contention inflated everything by about a fifth), the image's two runs made right after the JVM's passes and not interleaved with them, process CPU time including the compiler and collector threads: an indication.}}
\li{\strong{What decides it}: warm passes of the machine in the container, 8 workers and 1, pipelined load, on Init, Std, a Cedar-level corpus and mathlib, for the JVM launcher and the image (for the image "warm" removes only page faults and table growth). If the indication holds, the JVM launcher is the faster platform by the owner's criterion, at two to three times the memory - and the arena run, a cold process, still favours the image by the numbers of section I.2. Both cannot be had from one launcher today; the machine's compiled tier (rlvm-truffle) moves the same question into the image.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{I.8 What was changed, what was not measured, how to repeat}

\p{\strong{Changed in this wave} (all in the pipeline owner's modules):}

\ul{
\li{\src{scripts/build.sh}: \code{\verb~|--opt~} defaults to \strong{3} (it was 2), so \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh dist-native~} builds the image the arena build builds (request of the machine integrator; measured by him: -10 to -18 \% CPU for the machine, about -10 \% for the reference engine; the build takes 2.5 minutes and 900 CPU-seconds instead of 1 to 2 minutes and 500). \src{scripts/arena/build.sh} has used \code{\verb~|--opt 3~} since wave 4.}
\li{\src{scripts/build.sh}: \strong{Oracle GraalVM is used only on request} (\code{\verb~|--pgo~}; OWNER-NOTES item 11). Until today a default build made the PGO image whenever \code{\verb~|.toolchain/oracle-graalvm-25~} existed. No script downloads it. New \code{\verb~|--train-arg ARG~} passes arguments to the PGO training runs (\code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}).}
\li{\src{scripts/build.sh}: the shared distribution \code{\verb~|work/dist~} is \strong{versioned}: each build is installed as \code{\verb~|work/dist.d/<UTC time>-<sha256 prefix of the RLVM jars>-<jvm|native>~} and \code{\verb~|work/dist~} is a symbolic link switched by one \code{\verb~|rename(2)~}. \src{scripts/gate.sh}, \src{scripts/slice.sh}, \src{bench/track/track.py}, \code{\verb~|ladder.py~} and \src{scripts/platform-matrix.py} resolve the link once at their start. Old versions are deleted when they are neither among the newest four nor younger than 90 minutes. \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh dist-versions~} lists them. (The shared \code{\verb~|work/dist~} itself is still the plain directory of 12:17Z, \code{\verb~|-O2~}: the build that would have converted it was mine, ran without a host slot when the orchestrator introduced them, and was stopped; the next shared build converts it. The scheme was exercised on a scratch distribution and on a clean copy of the tree.)}
\li{\src{scripts/gate.sh}, \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh dist-native~} / \code{\verb~|dist-tree~}: they take the host-wide slot of \src{bench/slot.sh} themselves (\code{\verb~|heavy~}, respectively \code{\verb~|ni~}: at most two native-image builds on the host) unless the caller already holds one; \code{\verb~|gate.sh~} runs four files at a time by default (it was 8 and 10). The orchestrator's rule of 14:32Z, after the host had run out of memory.}
\li{\src{scripts/platform-matrix.py} finds slices (\code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~}); \src{scripts/platform-report.py} has the worker-scaling table (\code{\verb~|scaling:TAG~}). \src{bench/track/benchmarks.py} writes one RLVM column per engine and, since owner note 12, the cold wall clock before CPU time.}
\li{\code{\verb~|rlvm-api~} / \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~}: \code{\verb~|EngineProvider.runDefaults(Map)~} - the primary engine may prefer run settings (the machine's session cap, request of the machine integrator); applied under the command line. No provider declares one yet, so no run changed.}
}

\p{\strong{Not changed}: the default engine (reference; the machine registers with priority 10 and its workflow raises it), the parent pom's module list, the JVM launcher's flags, the image's collector and heap limit.}

\p{\strong{Not measured, and why}}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Warm passes in the container} (owner note 12; section I.7), \strong{the machine on mathlib} (either launcher), \strong{the machine on the JVM launcher beyond Std size}, and \strong{x86-64} (no such machine here).}
\li{\strong{Oracle GraalVM PGO with the machine}: it needs three more image builds under the build lock (\code{\verb~|work/build/.lock~}), for which six builds of other agents were waiting at 13:50Z and ten at 13:53Z. Part II has the PGO image for the reference engine (small corpora: 0.82 and 0.91 times the JVM launcher's CPU time with eight workers and with one, where the CE image needs 0.92 and 1.03; mathlib +2 \% against the JVM launcher, the CE image +12 \%). It is a measurement only in any case (owner's decision: nothing that ships uses Oracle GraalVM).}
\li{\strong{The machine's staging switches} (\code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, \code{\verb~|probes~}: -3 to -9 \% CPU in \code{\verb~|work/status/machine-integration.md~}): they are the machine workflow's to turn on; the soundness review found wrong accepts under \code{\verb~|probes~} and \code{\verb~|facts~} (13:48Z) and under \code{\verb~|warm~} (14:38Z; \code{\verb~|work/status/requests.log~}, findings S-F1, S-F2, S-F4), so v0 is the configuration that could ship today.}
\li{\strong{A quiet host, and a third repeat.} The load average was 26 to 95 during the two valid repeats; the absolute CPU times are a few percent above the wave-4 integrator's (Init, one worker: 7.94 s here, 7.77 s at load 7 to 10), the ratios inside a group are what the decision rests on. The third repeat, six workers, the nursery sizes, the JVM flags, the machine on the large corpora and the four-worker mathlib pair were all queued; after 13:50Z the host was out of memory, and the repetition (\code{\verb~|work/steward/w7/queue3.sh R~}, inside a host-wide slot as the orchestrator now requires) waited behind eight other queues for the lock and was withdrawn at 14:34Z, when it could no longer finish in this session.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh arena-test~}} (the arena definition built and run from a clean copy in the container) was not run after the changes to \src{scripts/build.sh}: it needs the same lock. Instead: a clean copy of the tree built by \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --jvm-only~} with the default (versioned) distribution, and the run line of \code{\verb~|rlvm.yaml~} with \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} substituted on three published tests through the \code{\verb~|work/dist~} link (accept / reject / accept as expected).}
}

\p{\strong{To repeat} (about 45 minutes of container time on a host that is not thrashing):}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- scripts/build.sh --published --no-pgo --opt 3 --dist work/steward/w7/dist   # frozen distribution
bench/slot.sh -- work/steward/w7/queue2.sh A B C D E F G   # the holds of the measurement lock (see the script)
work/steward/w7/assemble.py                    # this part: part1.md + scripts/platform-report.py --update
work/steward/w7/benchmarks.sh                  # docs/BENCHMARKS.md
~}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{Part II - the rehearsal with the reference engine (wave 4): cold processes, by CPU time}

\p{\em{Superseded as a decision by Part 0; kept for its measurements of the host JIT, the collectors and the heap sizes on cold processes of the reference engine.}}

\p{Steward, wave 4, 2026-11-24. Engine: the \strong{reference engine} (\code{\verb~|-X engine=reference -X load=batch~}), the only engine in the distribution at the time. This part was the rehearsal of the platform gate with the engine that existed; its scripts are the ones Part I ran again with the machine (section 7).}

\subtree{
\title{0. Summary}

\ol{
\li{\p{\strong{Launchers} (reference engine, jars of 07:54Z).}
\ul{
\li{\strong{mathlib: the JVM launcher needs the least CPU time.} Eight workers, the three launchers back to back: JVM 1,057 s, Oracle PGO image 1,082 s (+2 \%), CE native image 1,187 s (+12 \%); over the day the JVM took 926 to 1,057 s in six runs and the CE image 1,168 to 1,203 s in three (no overlap). Four workers: 946 s against 1,069 s (+13 \%). One worker: 886 s against 983 s (PGO, +11 \%) and 1,112 s (CE, +26 \%). Instructions: 6.3 to 6.5 T (JVM), 7.6 T (PGO), 9.0 T (CE).}
\li{\strong{Init, Std, cedar, reachability (12 to 55 s of CPU): within a tenth to a fifth of each other}, and the order depends on the worker count. CE image against JVM: 0.92 times the CPU time with eight workers (16 pairs), 1.03 times with one (12 pairs). PGO image: 0.82 and 0.91 times.}
\li{\strong{Memory}: the images need a third to a half of the JVM's RSS on the small corpora; on mathlib 8.7 GiB (CE) and 8.1 GiB (PGO) against 13.0 GiB with eight workers, 4.65 and 3.9 GiB against 11.8 GiB with one.}
}}
\li{\strong{JVM, host JIT}: C2 against Graal makes no difference worth a flag (0.97 and 1.01 times); C1 alone costs 22 \%. More compiler threads than the launcher's two look useful with eight workers (0.92 times the CPU time on the small corpora, eight pairs, range crossing 1.0): not changed, to be measured at the gate.}
\li{\strong{JVM, collector}: Serial, Parallel and G1 are within the noise in CPU time on the small corpora (zero to four young collections per run); Parallel and G1 need 17 to 89 \% more memory there, and on mathlib Parallel doubles the pauses and G1 has the highest RSS. Serial stays.}
\li{\strong{JVM, heap}: the process's RSS is its heap limit, not its need (the old generation fills with dead arrays before the first full collection; neither a growing heap nor a lower \code{\verb~|MinHeapFreeRatio~} changes that). With the jars of 07:54Z the live set on mathlib with eight workers is 5.5 to 9.1 GB: a 10 GB heap passed twice at no CPU cost (10.1 GiB RSS, six full collections of 0.1 to 0.3 s instead of three), a 9 GB heap ran out of memory - the native image's 8.7 GiB was out of reach. With the jars of 10:56Z (the store of 10:33Z) the live set is 3.1 to 4.5 GB: mathlib, con-leche, cslib, navier-stokes-euler and all 230 tests pass with \strong{8 GB} (mathlib: 7.9 GiB RSS, seven full collections), which is the native image's range.}
\li{\strong{Decisions.} \strong{No launcher default was changed.} The arena definition stays on the CE native image (description rewritten: reference engine; machine in progress); the JVM launcher keeps \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}. Reasons and what to do at the platform gate: section 6.3.}
\li{\strong{Caveat.} The host ran at three to nine times its capacity during the series (section 1.1), and during the wave the store's owner made the reference engine about 40 \% cheaper on mathlib (jar of 11:17Z, its owner's measurement), which is not in the measured distribution. Ratios between launchers were not re-measured with it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\ul{
\li{\p{\strong{Distribution.} A frozen copy with the three launchers built from the same jars, \code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/dist~} (\code{\verb~|BUILD-INFO.txt~} there lists every jar with its sha256 prefix):}
\pre{\verb~|
scripts/build.sh --published --pgo --opt 3 --dist work/steward/platform/dist
~}
\table{
\tr{\th{Launcher}\th{Configuration name}\th{What it is}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~}}\td{\code{\verb~|jvm~}}\td{the jars on HotSpot of GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 (JDK 25.0.5) with the launcher's default flags: \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~}}\td{\code{\verb~|native~}}\td{native image, GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial -R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}, 60 MB: what \src{scripts/arena/build.sh} builds}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-pgo~}}\td{\code{\verb~|pgo~}}\td{native image, \strong{Oracle GraalVM 25.0.4}, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~} + profile-guided optimisation, trained on \code{\verb~|init-prelude~} and \code{\verb~|init.ndjson~} with one worker, 42 MB. \strong{A measurement only}: Oracle GraalVM is under the GFTC licence, and whether the project may build with it is an open question with the project owner}}
}
\p{Jars: the integrator's final state of 07:35Z (driver \code{\verb~|b966222a51a9~}, ref \code{\verb~|9089b0a558f3~}, store \code{\verb~|90b8cf9432cb~}, load \code{\verb~|43a3adf4faaf~}, admit \code{\verb~|d89a73d8a715~}, inductive \code{\verb~|db8ac9802b19~}, api \code{\verb~|a956fdf3f0f4~}) with rlvm-cli \code{\verb~|f2af2657a704~} and rlvm-main \code{\verb~|080e6bcb2d6a~} of 07:54Z (public \code{\verb~|UsageException~}; no difference on the measured path). Later publications of this wave are not in it.}}
\li{\strong{Containers.} Eight workers: \code{\verb~|bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network~}, 8 performance cores (Cortex-X925, CPUs 5-9, 15-17 of this 20-core aarch64 host) and 16 GB, like the arena runner (x86-64, 8 vCPU, 16 GB). One worker: a container with the two remaining performance cores (CPUs 18, 19) and 16 GB - one worker thread, the loader before it and two JIT threads fit there, and these runs could then go on while other people measured in the eight. A one-worker row is therefore not "arena-shaped" in its core count; its CPU time is what it is about.}
\li{\strong{Units.} Peak RSS is printed in units of 2^20 and 2^30 bytes ("MB", "GB" in the tables are MiB and GiB); G instructions are 10^9.}
\li{\p{\strong{One run} = the arena run line of \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml} with the binary replaced, under \src{bench/measure.sh} (\code{\verb~|perf stat~} + GNU time around \code{\verb~|sh -c~}, exactly the arena's measurement):}
\pre{\verb~|
RLVM_STATUS_BASE=10 [RLVM_JAVA_OPTS='...'] DIST/bin/BINARY --threads N "$IN" -X engine=reference -X load=batch; case $? in ...
~}
\p{A JVM configuration other than \code{\verb~|jvm~} replaces \strong{all} VM flags through \code{\verb~|RLVM_JAVA_OPTS~}; the flags of every configuration are listed where it is used (\code{\verb~|scripts/platform-matrix.py --list~} prints all). Every JVM run also writes \code{\verb~|-Xlog:gc~} to a file; collection counts and pause totals come from it.}}
\li{\strong{Tools.} \code{\verb~|scripts/platform-matrix.py --configs A,B,.. --corpora .. --threads .. --repeat K --tag T~} runs a matrix and appends one JSON line per run (with the complete command) to \code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/results/T.jsonl~}; \code{\verb~|scripts/platform-all.sh STEP...~} is the series as run; \code{\verb~|scripts/platform-report.py --update docs/PLATFORM.md~} regenerates every table below from the result files. GC logs: \code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/gclogs/~}.}
\li{\strong{Interleaving and the lock.} For each repeat, corpus and worker count, every configuration runs once, back to back, in one container (a \em{group}), while the measurement lock of \src{bench/track/ladder.py} (\code{\verb~|work/perf-history/.ladder.lock~}) is held.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 The host was not quiet - how to read the numbers}

\p{Sixteen agents shared the host during the whole series (unit tests, guards, mutant campaigns, other measurements, other projects' jobs). The 1-minute load average is in the last column of every table: \strong{60 to 190 on 20 cores} for most runs (08:30Z to 11:00Z), 25 to 80 for the first mathlib run of the JVM launcher (08:01Z) and for the last groups (after 11:00Z). Other processes are not confined to the cores outside the containers, and other owners' measurement containers ran in the same eight cores without the lock during several groups (recorded per group in \code{\verb~|results/T.groups.jsonl~}: among them the back-to-back mathlib group, both heap groups and the four-worker mathlib group). Consequences:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{CPU time is inflated}, by an amount that changes from minute to minute (shared caches, memory bandwidth, clock frequency under an all-core load). The same binary on the same file: mathlib on the JVM launcher took 926 s of CPU at load 26 and 1,030 to 1,057 s at load 130 to 190 (+11 to +14 \%) with instruction counts 1.4 \% apart. The figure of a row is the \strong{minimum} over its runs, the median is in parentheses.}
\li{\strong{Compare configurations by the paired ratios}: runs of one group are minutes apart at most and saw nearly the same load. The ratio tables give the median over all groups of (configuration / reference configuration) and the range.}
\li{\strong{Wall clock} of eight-worker runs measures the host as much as the checker: it is listed, and it is not a basis for a decision here.}
\li{\strong{Peak RSS, collection counts, exit status and instructions} do not depend on the load.}
}

\p{For scale, on a quieter host (integration log, 07:35Z, same jars, container): Init JVM 15.1 s CPU / 12.4 s wall with one worker, 20.6 s / 3.9 s with eight; mathlib JVM 841.8 s CPU / 139.2 s wall / 13.1 GB with eight workers.}
}
}

