% <!-- END table:heap+heap2:-:-:jvm,jvm-heap-max-13g,jvm-heap-track-25,jvm-heap-track-15,jvm-heap-fixed-10g,jvm-heap-fixed-9g,jvm-g1-2-max-13g,jvm-parallel-2-max-13g,native-max-10g:plain -->

\p{What the GC logs say (mathlib, eight workers, Serial collector):}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The live set.} After a full collection the heap holds 4.1 to 4.4 GB at the end of the load (the two 10 GB runs, whose first full collection falls there) and 5.5 to 9.1 GB while eight workers check (42 full collections in nine Serial runs; above 8 GB four times: 8.1, 8.1, 8.7 and 9.1 GB, in four different runs). With one worker the native image's whole RSS is 4.65 GiB, with eight 8.7 GiB: about half a gigabyte of reachable arrays per worker comes on top of the store. The driver's memory account of such a run reported 3.9 GB (integration log): the rest is array capacity that is allocated and not in use, and worker memory outside the account - a figure for the store's and the driver's owners (the driver's jar of 09:39Z counts allocated capacity and gives memory back; it is not in the measured distribution), not something a VM flag changes.}
\li{\strong{RSS equals the heap limit.} The process touches the whole heap whatever the live set is: young collections promote dead arrays, and the Serial collector expands the old generation up to \code{\verb~|-Xmx~} before it runs its first full collection. So \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g~} (today), \code{\verb~|-Xmx13g~} alone (\code{\verb~|jvm-heap-max-13g~}) and a lower \code{\verb~|MinHeapFreeRatio~} (\code{\verb~|jvm-heap-track-15~}, \code{\verb~|-25~}: full collections at 87 s, 110 s, 131 s, each from 12.6 to 13.0 GB) all end at 13.0 to 13.1 GiB. With \strong{one} worker the JVM reaches 11.8 GiB without a single full collection, where the native image needs 4.65 GiB. The only setting that lowers the peak is the limit itself.}
\li{\strong{10 GB was enough twice, 9 GB is not.} \code{\verb~|-Xms10g -Xmx10g~}: accepted in both runs, 10.1 GiB RSS, six full collections instead of three, 0.09 to 0.34 s each (1.3 s per run), CPU time inside the spread of the 13 GB runs (940 and 992 s against 926 to 1,057 s). \code{\verb~|-Xms9g -Xmx9g~}: \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} after 337 s (exit status 3; ten full collections, the last two left 7.7 GB of the 8 GB old generation in use and an array did not fit). The 9.1 GB live set of the \code{\verb~|jvm-heap-track-25~} run would not have fitted into the 9 GB old generation of a 10 GB heap either.}
\li{\strong{A full collection is cheap here} (0.07 to 0.47 s for 4 to 9 GB of live primitive arrays: nothing to trace, little to move), which is why a smaller heap costs no measurable CPU time. Young collections: 81 to 86 per run, 3.8 to 4.8 s in total, whatever the heap size.}
\li{\strong{Other collectors on mathlib.} Parallel (2 threads): 155 young and 4 full collections, 10.4 s of pauses, same RSS, CPU time at the upper end of the Serial runs. G1 (2 + 1 threads): 0.7 s of pauses, no full collection, wall clock among the best of the hour - and 13.2 GiB RSS, the highest of all: it lets the heap fill before it reclaims the dead humongous arrays. Neither lowers RSS or CPU time.}
\li{\strong{The native image} needs 8.7 to 9.0 GiB with its default 13 GB limit and 7.9 GiB with \code{\verb~|-Xmx10g~} (same CPU time).}
}

\p{So, with the measured distribution (jars of 07:54Z): the smallest heap that did not cost CPU time on mathlib with eight workers is 10 GB (10.1 GiB RSS), and it has no margin: 9 GB fails, and the peaks of the live set move with the schedule (up to 9.1 GB seen, which a 10 GB heap cannot hold); a heap that is safe for every schedule seen needs 11 GB or more, and the native image's 8.7 to 9.4 GiB cannot be reached.}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 The same question with the jars of 10:56Z}

\p{During the wave the store's owner published a store whose overlays give memory back (10:33Z) and the driver's owner a memory account of allocated capacity with give-back (09:39Z). The store's owner reported mathlib accepted with \code{\verb~|-Xms8g -Xmx8g -Xmn1g~} at 8.24 GB RSS (requests.log, 11:00Z). Checked here with the distribution frozen at 10:56Z (\code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/dist-1100~}: store \code{\verb~|720f505913d7~}, driver \code{\verb~|d8bebed464b5~}, ref \code{\verb~|85ed3ee7a85a~}, admit \code{\verb~|8d7092222f70~}, load \code{\verb~|30617170f7a3~}), JVM launcher, Serial, eight workers. These runs were made on the \strong{host} (efficiency cores and CPUs 18, 19, beside other people's jobs, because the container's cores were taken): they are pass / fail, RSS and GC-log results, \strong{not timings}.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Input}\th{Heap}\th{Result}\th{Peak RSS}\th{Collections (young / full)}\th{Live after a full collection}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{\code{\verb~|-Xms10g -Xmx10g -Xmn1g~}}\td{accept}\td{10.02 GiB}\td{132 / 4}\td{3.2 to 4.4 GB}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{\code{\verb~|-Xms8g -Xmx8g -Xmn1g~}}\td{accept}\td{7.86 GiB}\td{126 / 7 (0.04 to 0.38 s each, 1.2 s)}\td{3.1 to 4.5 GB}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{8 / 10 / 13 GB}\td{accept}\td{7.41 / 7.29 / 7.28 GiB}\td{-}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{8 / 10 / 13 GB}\td{accept}\td{3.73 / 3.40 / 3.48 GiB}\td{-}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{8 / 10 / 13 GB}\td{accept}\td{2.50 / 2.36 / 2.48 GiB}\td{-}\td{-}}
\tr{\td{all 230 published tests (one worker)}\td{8 GB and 10 GB}\td{230 / 230 correct, 0 declined (verdict runs)}\td{-}\td{-}\td{-}}
}

\p{(\code{\verb~|work/logs/steward/heap-current-jars.log~}, \code{\verb~|heap-current-jars-mathlib.log~}, \code{\verb~|work/steward/slice-all-heap-8g.md~}, \code{\verb~|slice-all-heap-10g.md~}, GC logs \code{\verb~|work/steward/platform/gclogs/host-current-jars-*~}.) con-leche is the input with the largest need: 7.3 GiB whatever the limit (one declaration, unit 24018).}

\p{\strong{Answer to the two questions of the task.} The smallest heap that costs no CPU time: 10 GB with the jars of this morning (no margin), 8 GB with the jars of 10:56Z (everything above passes; the extra full collections cost about a second on mathlib). The native image's RSS (8.7 to 9.4 GiB on mathlib): out of reach this morning, reached with \code{\verb~|-Xmx8g~} now (7.9 GiB). \strong{The default was not lowered}: the smallest safe heap moved by 2 GB within three hours as the store changed, it will move again when the machine is the engine, an \code{\verb~|OutOfMemoryError~} is a lost verdict while RSS is not ranked, and with 13 GB the process stays below 13.2 GiB on a 16 GB runner. The setting to take to the platform gate is \code{\verb~|-Xms10g -Xmx10g -Xmn1g~}, verified against the engine that is shipped then.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Reading and recommendation}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 Launchers}

\ul{
\li{\strong{mathlib decides for the JVM launcher on CPU time.} Eight workers, back to back: JVM 1,057 s, PGO image 1,082 s (+2 \%), CE image 1,187 s (+12 \%). Over the whole day the JVM launcher with a 13 GB Serial heap took 926 to 1,057 s in six eight-worker runs (940 and 992 s with a 10 GB heap) and the CE image 1,168 to 1,203 s in three: the two ranges do not overlap, at any load. One worker: 886 s against 983 s (PGO, +11 \%) and 1,112 s (CE, +26 \%). Instruction counts, which do not depend on the load: 6.3 to 6.5 T against 7.6 T and 9.0 T (CE: +41 \%). This is decision 12 of ARCHITECTURE.md ("the JIT produces better code than the CE AOT compiler") measured on the real pipeline: the advantage is 11 to 26 \% in CPU time for the reference engine, and it is larger with one worker than with eight, where the JVM's own threads (JIT, collector) and its warm-up are multiplied.}
\li{\strong{On the small corpora the three are within a tenth to a fifth of each other}, and the order turns around with the worker count: the CE image needs 0.92 times the JVM's CPU time with eight workers (median of 16 pairs; the JVM warms up eight threads at once and compiles while they run) and 1.03 times with one. The PGO image needs 0.82 and 0.91 times. A run of 15 to 55 s is where warm-up and steady state balance for this engine. The order of the runs inside a group is not what makes the difference: with the JVM first the medians are 0.93 and 0.83 (12 pairs), with the JVM last 0.86 and 0.78 (4 pairs, at a lower host load).}
\li{\strong{Four workers on mathlib} (the arena run line's count): JVM 946 s CPU / 266 s wall / 13.0 GiB, CE image 1,069 s (+13 \%) / 295 s / 7.5 GiB.}
\li{\strong{Memory is the image's side}: a third to a half of the JVM's RSS on the small corpora; on mathlib 8.7 GiB (CE) and 8.1 GiB (PGO) against 13.0 GiB, and 4.65 and 3.9 GiB against 11.8 GiB with one worker. Section 5: with these jars the JVM cannot go below about 10 GiB on mathlib with eight workers; with the store of 10:33Z it can (7.9 GiB with an 8 GB heap).}
\li{\strong{PGO} is the best of both where it was trained (Init) and close to the JVM on mathlib with eight workers, at the image's memory. It needs Oracle GraalVM (GFTC licence): not a candidate until the project owner has decided; the numbers are here so that the decision has them.}
\li{The 230 small tests are not ranked and have no tight limits; the image answers them in milliseconds, the JVM launcher in 0.2 to 0.4 s each. \code{\verb~|perf/app-lam~}, the test closest to its limit, is the other way round: 9.3 s on the JVM launcher against 16 s for the image (integration log).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 JVM flags}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Host JIT.} Graal (libgraal) instead of C2 as the JVM's own compiler changes little: 0.97 times C2's CPU time with eight workers (eight pairs, range 0.93 to 1.07) and 1.01 with one (eight pairs, 0.97 to 1.05), with 11 to 18 \% more instructions. C1 alone costs 22 \% more CPU time (one worker). \strong{C2 stays.} The number of compiler threads is the one flag that showed an effect: with HotSpot's own choice (four on eight CPUs) instead of the launcher's \code{\verb~|-XX:CICompilerCount=2~}, eight workers leave the interpreter and the C1 code earlier, and the run needs 0.92 times the CPU time and 0.90 times the wall clock (median of eight pairs; Init, Std and cedar 7 to 11 \% less in both repeats, reachability 8 \% more; range 0.82 to 1.08). The flag dates from the time when instructions were the target ("compiler threads are billed", \src{scripts/build.sh}); by CPU time with parallel workers it looks wrong for runs of this size. \strong{Not changed today}: eight pairs on a loaded host with a range that crosses 1.0 are not unambiguous, the one-worker case and mathlib were not measured, and the JVM launcher is not the shipped binary. It is the first thing to measure at the gate.}
\li{\strong{Collector.} On Init, Std, cedar and reachability with eight workers a run sees zero to four young collections and no full one, whatever the collector (the nursery is 1 GB; G1 often collects nothing at all), so the collector has little to decide: Parallel with two threads needs 0.97 times Serial's CPU time (median of eight pairs, range 0.89 to 1.17), G1 with 2 + 1 threads 0.99 (0.86 to 1.13), the uncapped variants 0.96 and 0.99 - ranges that are the noise of this host. Every alternative needs more memory: +17 \% RSS (Parallel, two threads), +23 to +24 \% (G1), +89 \% (Parallel uncapped: eight collector threads' buffers and an adaptive nursery). On mathlib (section 5) Parallel doubles the collections and the pause time, and G1 has the highest RSS of all configurations. \strong{Serial stays.} (The wall-clock ratios of that table, 0.84 to 0.87 for all four alternatives alike, are not explained by the pause totals, which are a few hundred milliseconds either way; the Serial configuration ran first in every group. The launcher comparison, repeated in both orders, shows no advantage of a later position, so this stays unexplained and is not used.)}
\li{\strong{Heap.} Section 5. \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~} stays for now: it is the limit under which no schedule of any jar set of today ran out of memory, and the process cannot grow beyond about 13.2 GiB with it. \code{\verb~|-Xms10g -Xmx10g -Xmn1g~} is the candidate for the gate (no CPU cost; 2 GB above what today's jars need), 8 GB the floor with today's jars.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Recommendation}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Do not switch the arena definition now; switch it at the platform gate if the picture holds.} The definition keeps the CE native image. For mathlib-class inputs the JVM launcher is the faster platform for the reference engine by a margin that the noise of this host does not explain away (non-overlapping ranges, 11 to 26 \%, instructions 29 \% lower), and by the project's order of criteria (CPU time, wall clock, memory) it would be the one to ship for mathlib. What speaks against switching today: (a) the binding gate is defined for the machine (RECONCILIATION section 3: "run when the machine first accepts Init"), whose balance of compiled code against substitution and interning is a different one - the reference engine is not what will be submitted; (b) every number here is aarch64 on a host at five to nine times its capacity; the runner is x86-64 and quiet; (c) the measured distribution is this morning's - the store published at 11:17Z makes the reference engine about 40 \% cheaper on mathlib (its owner's measurement), and nobody has measured how the launchers compare with it; (d) the JVM's 13 GiB on a 16 GB runner leaves less room than the image's 9 GiB (the one arena failure on record that was a kill by the VM, rpylean, exit 137, happened at 14.65 GB, docs/research/01), and the heap that brings the JVM to 8 to 10 GiB is safe only since this morning's store change (section 5.1); (e) \src{bench/rlvm/run.sh} (not the steward's) finds the binary in the run line by the text \code{\verb~|work/dist/bin/rlvm ~} and would need a change with it. The switch itself is one word in \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml} (\code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~} to \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~}; the build step already produces both).}
\li{\strong{Re-run this series when the machine passes its gates}, with the machine as the engine (\code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM_ENGINE=machine~}), on a quiet host, mathlib three times per launcher, and once on an x86-64 machine: \src{scripts/platform-all.sh} + \code{\verb~|scripts/platform-report.py --update docs/PLATFORM.md~} (section 7). Decide then by CPU time, wall clock, memory, in that order.}
\li{\strong{Until then, measure and guard on the JVM launcher} (what \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh dist~} builds in four seconds; lowest CPU time on the large corpora) and keep the image for the arena definition and the small tests.}
\li{\strong{Memory is the engine's problem before it is the platform's.} This morning: 4 GB of store plus half a gigabyte per worker of reachable arrays, of which the memory account saw 3.9 GB; no heap flag brought the JVM below 10 GiB. After the store's change of 10:33Z: 3.1 to 4.5 GB live, and 8 GB of heap are enough. The heap limit only decides how much dead memory the process keeps.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. What was changed, what was not, and how to repeat this}

\p{\strong{Launcher defaults: none was changed.} Not the launcher of the arena definition (CE native image, \code{\verb~|--threads 4~}), not the JVM launcher's flags (\src{scripts/build.sh}: Serial, C2, two compiler threads, \code{\verb~|-Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~}), not the image's (\code{\verb~|-O2~} for \code{\verb~|dev.sh dist-native~}, \code{\verb~|-O3~} for the arena build, \code{\verb~|--gc=serial~}, \code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}). The candidates for a change at the platform gate, in the order of the evidence for them: the JVM launcher for mathlib-class inputs (6.3), a 10 GB heap for it (5.1), HotSpot's own compiler thread count (6.2).}

\p{Changed in the tools (all in \code{\verb~|scripts/~}, the steward's):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --published --pgo~} works: the Oracle variant compiles \code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~} from the snapshot as well, so that its Truffle jars follow the Oracle GraalVM's version (it used to pick the CE Truffle jars through the published \code{\verb~|rlvm-cli~} pom, and \code{\verb~|native-image~} failed in \code{\verb~|TruffleBaseFeature~}). The verification of every launcher now also checks the usage status (64).}
\li{New: \src{scripts/platform-matrix.py} (the matrix; \code{\verb~|--list~} for the configurations, \code{\verb~|--cpuset~} for the one-worker container, records the host load and other containers on its CPUs, stops its container when killed), \src{scripts/platform-all.sh} (the series), \src{scripts/platform-report.py} (the tables of this file; \code{\verb~|--update docs/PLATFORM.md~}). \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh platform ...~} and \code{\verb~|platform-report~}.}
\li{\src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}: description and state comment rewritten (reference engine; the machine is in progress and is not in the binary); the run line is unchanged. \src{scripts/arena/test-in-docker.sh} holds the measurement lock while it builds and runs. \code{\verb~|scripts/dev.sh arena-test --reuse --strict~} passes with the tree of 10:26Z (work/status/steward.log).}
}

\p{Not measured, and why:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64.} No such machine here. The baselines document (docs/research/12) has aarch64 / x86-64 ratios for other checkers, none for HotSpot against a native image.}
\li{\strong{A quiet host.} See 1.1. The absolute CPU times are 10 to 25 \% above what the same binaries need on an idle host; the ratios between configurations of one group are the result.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|-march=native~}} for the image (ARCHITECTURE 7.5 calls it admissible), \strong{\code{\verb~|-O2~} against \code{\verb~|-O3~}} (the integration log's \code{\verb~|-O2~} image needed 10,207 G instructions on mathlib, the \code{\verb~|-O3~} image here 9,025 G: -12 \%; no CPU-time pair), \strong{the epsilon collector} and \strong{G1 for the image} (Oracle only): each needs another image build of 700 CPU-seconds, which this host could not give today.}
\li{\strong{ZGC / Shenandoah} on the JVM: concurrent collectors could hold the heap near the live set at the price of collector threads; one mathlib run each would tell.}
\li{\strong{Three repeats of mathlib per launcher} (ARCHITECTURE 7.1 asks for three at the gate): one back-to-back group plus the single runs listed; the heap and collector rows are single runs.}
\li{\strong{Four workers} on the small corpora, and the default worker count itself: the driver's owner measures that (\src{rlvm-driver/bin/threads-gate.sh}).}
}

\p{To repeat everything (about three hours of an idle host):}

\pre{\verb~|
scripts/build.sh --published --pgo --opt 3 --dist work/steward/platform/dist              # frozen distribution
scripts/platform-all.sh small8 mathlib8 heap heap2 jit8 jit8-1w gc8 mathlib4 jvm-mathlib  # arena-shaped container
scripts/platform-all.sh small1 jit1 mathlib1                                              # one worker, CPUs 18,19
scripts/platform-report.py --update docs/PLATFORM.md                                      # the tables
~}

\p{With the machine: \code{\verb~|RLVM_PLATFORM_ENGINE=machine scripts/platform-all.sh ...~} (the machine's jar must be in the distribution, i.e. the module in the parent pom; for the JVM launcher alone \code{\verb~|RLVM_EXTRA_CP~} is enough). On a host with other core numbers: \code{\verb~|RLVM_SPARE_CPUS=a,b~} for the one-worker container.}
}
}
